We present a systematic philosophical framework treating mathematics as a complete theological system. Drawing on mathematical Platonism, model theory, Gödel's incompleteness theorems, and category theory, we develop formal analogues for traditional theological concepts: mathematical objects as eternal forms, proof as revelation, incompleteness as mystery, and category-theoretic universality as transcendence. This framework is not metaphorical but structural—we argue that mathematical practice exhibits all essential features of religious practice, including faith (axiom acceptance), revelation (proof discovery), community (peer review), and transformation (understanding). We explore implications for epistemology, ethics, and meaning-making, proposing that mathematical theology offers a rigorous foundation for spiritual experience grounded in demonstrable truth rather than culturally contingent narrative.
\end{abstract}

