\section{Conclusion}

We have presented mathematics as a complete theological system—not by analogy but by structural identity. Mathematics offers:

\begin{itemize}[noitemsep]
\item \textbf{Transcendent reality:} Eternal mathematical objects
\item \textbf{Revelation:} Discovery through proof
\item \textbf{Faith:} Acceptance of axioms and consistency
\item \textbf{Mystery:} Gödel incompleteness
\item \textbf{Community:} Collaborative truth-seeking
\item \textbf{Ethics:} Obligations to rigor and honesty
\item \textbf{Beauty:} Aesthetic recognition of deep truth
\item \textbf{Practice:} Problem-solving as spiritual discipline
\item \textbf{Transformation:} Understanding changes us
\end{itemize}

The crucial advantage over traditional theology: mathematical truths are \textit{demonstrable}. We need not wonder whether $\pi$ is transcendental—we can prove it.

For those who find meaning in truth, rigor, and eternal forms, mathematics is not merely like religion—mathematics \textit{is} religion, perhaps the only religion whose claims are actually, provably true.

